---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const two = assertion('two-of-ten-high-schools-offer-it')
const ohioHalf = assertion('half-of-ohios-high-schools-and-a-fifth-of-this-countys')
const rank = assertion('lima-senior-is-nineteenth-largest-of-the-schools-without-it')
const series = assertion('six-four-four-five-two')
const perry = assertion('perry-dropped-physics-and-calculus')
const supplied = assertion('better-supplied-than-ohio-on-everything-but-one')
const gifted = assertion('four-of-ten-report-a-gifted-programme')
const certified = assertion('the-county-beat-ohio-on-certification-by-a-point')
const allenEast = assertion('allen-east-taught-half-its-classes-uncertified')
const retired = assertion('the-certification-question-was-retired-after-2017')

const three = assertion('three-black-pupils-in-an-advanced-placement-course')
const required = assertion('the-required-courses-are-taken-evenly')
const elective = assertion('the-elective-courses-are-not')
const building = assertion('most-of-the-black-pupils-are-in-the-school-with-no-ap')
const standard = assertion('the-gap-survives-standardising-on-the-school')
const split = assertion('between-schools-multiplies-by-one-point-six-and-inside-by-four-point-six')
const lima = assertion('inside-lima-the-ratio-is-five-to-one')
const science = assertion('the-county-takes-more-science-than-ohio')
const quarter = assertion('a-quarter-of-ohios-advanced-placement')

const ccp = assertion('four-hundred-and-ninety-two-sat-a-college-course')
const pays = assertion('the-state-pays-for-one-and-the-family-for-the-other')
const spencerville = assertion('nearly-half-of-spencerville-is-in-a-college-course')
const least = assertion('the-largest-high-school-uses-it-least')
const bath = assertion('bath-went-from-one-hundred-and-ninety-nine-to-none')
const race = assertion('white-pupils-are-three-and-a-half-times-as-likely')

const fell = assertion('advanced-placement-fell-by-half-and-by-nine-tenths')
const ratio = assertion('the-ratio-went-from-one-point-four-to-seven-point-six')
const lima2013 = assertion('lima-enrolled-sixty-seven-in-2013')
const changed = assertion('the-four-that-changed-did-not-change-together')
const stayed = assertion('the-pupils-did-not-leave')
const substitution = assertion('the-substitution-replaced-a-quarter-of-the-seats')

const requiredSpec = bar(
  required.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  required.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Per cent of the group enrolled', tickSuffix: '%', labelValues: true },
)
const electiveSpec = bar(
  elective.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  elective.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Per cent of the group enrolled', tickSuffix: '%', labelValues: true },
)
const ohioSpec = bar(
  quarter.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  quarter.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Per cent of high-school pupils', tickSuffix: '%', labelValues: true },
)
const standardSpec = bar(
  standard.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  standard.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Black pupils enrolled', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="where-the-choosing-starts">
  <p>
    A county's schools can be measured two ways. One asks what its children score and how many of
    them are suspended. The other asks what they were offered — whether the building holds a
    calculus class at all, whether anyone teaches physics, whether a pupil who wants a college
    credit can get one without leaving town. The second measure is a federal collection, school by
    school and by race, and it is the half of the record that goes unread.
  </p>

  <h2>What is on offer</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={two} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ohioHalf} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={rank} />
  <p>
    A high school of nine hundred and eighty-four pupils that offers no Advanced Placement course is
    unusual in Ohio and not unheard of, and the company it keeps is instructive: online academies,
    career centres, and the high schools of Lorain, Euclid, Dayton and Columbus. Every one of them
    serves a city that has lost people and industry.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={series} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={supplied} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="ohio"
    title="Advanced Placement and gifted programmes, Allen County against Ohio"
    caption="Per cent of high-school pupils enrolled, 2021–22, over every Ohio school with a twelfth grade."
    columns={['Measure', 'Per cent']}
    rows={quarter.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="ohio" set:html={JSON.stringify(ohioSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={quarter} />
  <p>
    The pattern is sharp enough to be worth stating plainly. On every course a district staffs out
    of its own budget and examines itself, this county is at or above the state. On the one whose
    credit is granted elsewhere, by an examination a family pays a fee to sit, it is at a quarter of
    the state. That is a correlation and not a mechanism, and the file carries no reason.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={perry} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={gifted} />

  <h2>Who is teaching it</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={certified} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={allenEast} />
  <p>
    Six of the ten schools were at ninety-four per cent or above. One was at half, and the classes
    it staffed with a teacher certified in something else include its only calculus class and five
    of its seven algebra I classes.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={retired} />

  <h2>Who takes them</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={three} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={required} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="required"
    title="The courses the state requires"
    caption="Physics, geometry and algebra II, as a share of each group's high-school enrolment, 2021–22."
    columns={['Course and group', 'Per cent']}
    rows={required.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="required" set:html={JSON.stringify(requiredSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={elective} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="elective"
    title="The courses a pupil elects"
    caption="Chemistry and Advanced Placement, as a share of each group's high-school enrolment, 2021–22."
    columns={['Course and group', 'Per cent']}
    rows={elective.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="elective" set:html={JSON.stringify(electiveSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Every course on which the two groups are level is one a pupil is carried into by the graduation
    requirement. Every course on which they are not is one a pupil, a parent or a counsellor has to
    ask for. The file records who ended up enrolled and nothing about who asked, so what follows is
    a shape rather than a cause.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={science} />

  <h2>Two gaps, not one</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={building} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={standard} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="standardised"
    title="Black pupils expected and enrolled, standardising on the school"
    caption="Each school's own white rate applied to its own Black enrolment, against what the file records."
    columns={['Measure', 'Pupils']}
    rows={standard.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="standardised" set:html={JSON.stringify(standardSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={split} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={lima} />
  <p>
    The two halves point at different institutions. What happens inside a building is a district's
    business — who is advised into a course, who is scheduled against it, who is asked for money.
    Which building a child is in is a matter of district lines, open enrolment and where a family
    can afford to live, and no counselling office reaches it. Both are true here and the larger of
    the two is the first.
  </p>

  <h2>The county's other route</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={ccp} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={pays} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={spencerville} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={least} />
  <p>
    Nearly half of Spencerville High School is enrolled in a college course and one pupil in twenty
    at Lima Senior is. The programme is the same programme in both buildings, paid for the same way
    by the same statute, and the two counts are 121 of 252 and 48 of 984.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={race} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={bath} />

  <h2>Where it went</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={fell} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ratio} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={lima2013} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={changed} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={stayed} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={substitution} />
  <p>
    Three explanations fit the file equally well and it separates none of them: the advanced pupils
    moved to College Credit Plus, the certified teachers left, or too few pupils elected the courses
    to fill a section. The substitution story is the strongest and it does not close the question,
    because the county gained thirty-four college-course places against a hundred and thirty-eight
    Advanced Placement ones lost, and at the school where most of the loss sits the college-course
    count went from forty-nine to forty-eight.
  </p>
</Article>
